** Adding Depth

That definitely brought it closer to something that can have clear argument. But I also am realizing that public experts are also a form of [[id:23efaf4f-f79d-4992-b7bd-26044aaabb73][influencer]], [[id:78183679-67ee-4ece-9a37-4c8a1e9cd01f][inspired]] by [[https://www.reddit.com/r/CraiticalTheory/comments/1n4edvs/influencers_and_foucaults_theory_of_confession/][this post]] on the [[id:8347a91e-4dbb-4ae4-8550-6f0a1a21eb9b][CriticalTheory subreddit]].

OP [[id:22a32b15-f24a-4d4b-89c1-4233b7cc3856][postulates]] that influencers operate through [[id:37d7a271-8f85-43f7-847b-acbe6d41f63a][confession]], connecting it to [[id:e7d11cd2-1c74-406e-960e-0d56f90e5331][Foucault]]'s [[id:8b75205b-aed0-4752-8e7a-782142b8030d][theory of confession]].

[[id:228bc1ae-49ec-402c-a14e-3ab5d8933a61][The History of Sexuality]] [[id:bf3f18be-7c8a-451f-b777-29c253eca508][maps]] how [[id:df98ab7c-4342-45ee-96ab-688353d94947][modern forms of power]] rely less on [[id:723c98c3-0dd4-4580-b113-aa0b80805d3d][prohibition]] and more on [[id:94b7740d-cc33-4989-b477-b262d5d6d3cf][incitement to discourse]]: that is, they induce people talking about themselves, their desires, and beliefs: this lines up pretty well with how modern [[id:e43a01a6-99f3-4c34-8d1c-2ac4c60c2cfe][social media influencers]] operate. (And helps me see that perhaps rather than "public expert," I am trying to refer to "social media expert," though that is ambiguously an expert /of/ or /on/ social media.

Explaining Foucault's confession more: confession is an [[id:1e01ffa0-bd7e-46fe-b7a9-9354fc152715][act of subjection]], that is, an [[id:32e00173-c695-4bb6-b0d2-9dc17fec128b][act]] that makes the [[id:c41a47ed-c509-4d25-9f52-dca7f0835663][penitent]] a [[id:4695cc5c-70e9-442f-8a89-4b14fff5be3e][subject]] to the [[id:3f6b13e4-a2eb-40e7-b7dc-208bb01b814a][ideology]] [[id:6ac7d96e-bb26-4197-a6df-0f42d81778b3][confessor]]. Related ideas: [[id:3fd7d0b4-59cf-42c2-9573-a7d869599f7b][pastoral power]].

An important part of Foucault's theory is that there is [[id:783b14ff-cebb-4a00-8095-ba460e1d9528][subjection]][fn:: originally "assujettissement" in Foucault's original [[id:80ad4345-3276-4d1d-9d02-cbcd794c7815][French]], sometimes "subjectification" in English scholarship] and [[id:e56a685f-e87f-47e7-900b-610e9fb2f436][subjectivation]].

Subjection may be described as the process of systems inducing, cohering, and stabilizing an individual as a subject, while subjectivzation may be described as the process of how individuals cohere and stabilize themselves as subjects of those systems.

The reddit thread, someone shares some other resources.

I've heard of the [[id:aede7408-01df-4f8f-b93c-b210433fe7bd][back stage]] and [[id:a6c2654f-cc83-46df-aa15-0ebb70d2e28a][front stage]] [[id:c9cc4a84-0f4a-4bd6-8ebe-5048131187bc][bifurcation]] that's in [[id:153b6050-36f4-4938-a3a8-be96c7334670][Erving Goffman]]'s [[id:ec0b504e-3bc4-4a9e-af4e-04713fcbef16][dramaturgical theory]] before; I feel like a lot of people have a relatively intuitive understanding of that concept, though the theory definitely adds texture to the its shape.

Basically, there is a /front stage/, where performing occurs, and a /back stage/, where performance is planned and structured.

Some influencers complicate this split: their front stage is where they are performing a... scripted? performance for their [[id:d9b29a5d-b056-4d3e-bfa5-0af6321f3ce6][audience]], and their /back stage/, where influencers are performing personal life for their audience.

I'm trying to point to things like a beauty brand representative uploading a video where they're crying about their girlfriend in messy room, or the less caustic, homesteader who normally talks about cultivating tomatoes shows themselves walking their dog on the beach.

Both types of video from each person is a performance, but of a very different sort: one performs the front stage, the other the back. The influencer isn't aiming for [[id:33fa1a3f-d272-41cb-a6f0-ce86b90b1d4d][intimacy]], but an [[id:da69a023-831f-4181-a22d-ca332ddc3f12][illusion of intimacy]]. I don't think I've said it yet, but the term [[id:d6e95579-8de0-467f-9927-d70249ca7bbf][parasocial]] needs to get a mention.

I also see here a sort of cascading confession, because social media influencers are not just [[id:31a273df-bbbc-4000-9042-e99577deb010][broadcasting]] content: their audience is encouraged to engage... that is, confess, themselves. So the influencer and [[id:945a74ca-faa1-4ce4-8865-9906de553bc5][influencer audience member]] are both incited to confess to each other, by the social media system.

What this means for the pentinent/confessor relations is worth examining, especially from a [[id:09d5c15c-4d44-4efa-86ae-308f584a086c][Catholic]] perspective, given the origins of [[id:c827d36d-017c-4a34-b862-e666292d8895][confessing]] (as I'm thinking about it) in the [[id:3d508f83-dac3-43e2-8f42-5ac498a4be41][Church]].

Moving on for now, I'm also previously familiar with [[id:662e733d-5b6b-4498-a1a7-311ad832bec0][Mari Lehto]], for a few reasons, but it feels most important to share, because of [[id:73c4bd59-fe0e-4703-9a3a-dc184747f322][my introspection]] on [[id:8437966e-3f0a-4d9b-89e6-bac290b37fbf][my mental health]]: [[id:36a1cd4e-fd33-43cf-92ef-0130c2e62a07][anxiety]] is something I [[id:e78f958d-6f12-4bd0-a324-822252c23661][feel]] frequently, and I am [[id:82784ec3-2b50-400e-8f17-e545f140ee96][curious]] about why. But I also think that there is a lot of important things to look at in the domain of social media influencers, especially as it relates to [[id:61f7f4a0-8e58-4f1a-9114-30e296301176][Marxism]], [[id:16cad75c-c051-4f6d-8787-d8195b6701a0][radicalism]], and [[id:79e37cba-5d04-4406-893a-b42d319e322a][counter-insurrectionary programs]].

Lehto connected [[id:29c0984f-d1a0-42d7-8a09-47582d36a11e][affect theory]] with [[id:33b2053c-0020-4d53-bd7a-611da021a8d4][influencer theory]] to come up with a few ideas:

- The [[id:5ec32b29-5cc5-4be6-9edd-05afd2bc837a][affective practice of anxiety]]
  - Drawing on:
    - [[id:5951880c-0db2-4364-8af8-b4f1009cf43c][Margaret Wetherell]] and [[id:5bd932d2-99fe-449b-9ed8-07570d3b561d][affective practice]]
      - coined [[id:5bd932d2-99fe-449b-9ed8-07570d3b561d][affective practice]] as the [[id:4ffa4705-14b4-4854-ae34-0bec397ac417][embodied]] [[id:081d1a18-c09a-4ca5-8cad-e962bf25168e][routine]] of [[id:bbb0df8c-b366-4821-a552-1407280c6d9a][doing]] [[id:a7eaa4d6-016e-458d-853f-1d9336df0c11][emotionalism]]
    - [[id:a6051522-fe0b-4256-9c2a-b1636e973000][Arlie Russell Hochschild]]'s work on [[id:978132e0-5051-49b3-8aba-2e6d50ca5b2f][emotional labor]]
      - coined the idea [[id:6769d415-a993-422e-8ee2-7abf82260e79][feeling rules]]
  - THe affective practice of anxiety is what Finnish influencer mothers do produce and perform a self that is:
    - "agreeable to their followers"
    - "decrease[s] the discrepancy between their felt emotions and cultural expectations"

So, feeling rules lead to a practice of performing feelings in specific ways.

Lehto also draws on [[id:4a19210e-8904-4f51-b161-d0c6c7d65c87][Vicky Loveday]] and her idea of the [[id:54677d1d-cc41-481f-b656-7d03ec075105][neurotic academic]], which talks about how [[id:13e446c0-c09b-4e9c-930a-2a95b7a45fdc][academic persons]] in (neo)[[id:43f2c2e1-eb56-4f49-a6de-b9a6c212d115][liberal universities]] [[id:046e0943-cea7-451f-9c3e-38d5ffb034b5][experience]] and perform anxiety.

Lehto uses this to coin the term [[id:166f31db-cf25-4a5b-bdfe-059da2c66ac4][neurotic influencer]]

I can connect this to my own [[id:7031b7c2-7bfd-410e-8ed7-fbfc81dc3afc][relational dynamics]], in a way: the neurotic influencer is the [[id:133d3d41-f2c6-42dc-92f8-f5d904ccf88f][relational structure]] that holds the [[id:dd5e6bac-f266-4604-8d26-15771afd45e7][relational conditions]] of [[id:6769d415-a993-422e-8ee2-7abf82260e79][feeling rules]], which shape the [[id:02558c8c-ed20-48ea-8adb-07b63b24209b][relational acts]] of the [[id:6f97f130-c318-4180-8992-a465b75fa0db][neurotic influencer anxiety]], a [[id:1d2c1f2a-a0f8-4efd-a47e-58ef1b966416][mode of anxiety]].

I'd argue that the neurotic influencer is actually a mode of the neurotic academic, as social media is itself a mode of dicourse that in the relevant ways is functionally identical to academia. Put another way, while the neurotic academic is clearly a [[id:9928caaa-594e-414c-9509-dea5496ce124][traditional intellectual]] ([[id:b0f85824-6884-4f22-a7df-f2b6b9788ff9][Gramsci]]), it also may be true that the social media influencer is an [[id:cbde5651-0374-4303-8c82-cd5d496ae066][organic intellectual]]. This is an I explored recently in [[id:3084d46e-68d6-46dc-850d-343a0dfb86a9]["Citing for Containment"]]

There is, it seems, a way to construct the concept of "[[id:4360a3e7-109a-4838-8483-7adc34ddf316][neurotic intellectual]]," but I'm not ready for that logical slog yet.

I'm going to move on, perhaps too early, with confidence that it is better to build the field of ideas, at this point, than perfectly cultivate what I've established.

So, crisis induces learning, which in the case of public intellectuals on social media combines with the induction of organic intellectualism and neurotic performance by social media, induces a form of influencer performance that is founded in being a the neurotic influencer form of organic intellectual and the neurotic academic form of platformed intellectual.

I'll call it the [[id:2b8d9bab-9217-4d85-9aba-162d5a51ff17][neurotic platform intellectual]], realizing I haven't yet coined the term [[id:cb29f1f6-69d7-46a0-a5d3-407a60691a5d][platform intellectual]] - though maybe it'd be more properly "platformed" individual, or maybe even "platformal intellectual"? That actually might be it...

(At this point, the jargon is so tightly woven that I can feel myself missing, but revisions of these ideas should clean it up. Personally, as someone in the position being mapped, this all /feels/ somewhat true, even if poorly-expressed.)

